h are preserved. A `|` in the reason is kept as prose and escaped on the next run.\n  38 Re-running the gate keeps every row it can. A row the current review no longer reports is kept and its Source cell flagged, so a finding does not leave this record silently. ONE exception: when a finding id is REUSED by a different finding, the earlier decision cannot keep a row — the id is taken — and it is dropped. A RECORDED decision (anything but `open`) is named on the console when that happens; a row still at `open` is replaced silently, because `open` records no decision to lose.\n=== .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md\n   1 ---\n   2 phase: 01-retry-budget\n   3 reviewed: 2026-10-21T00:00:00Z\n   4 depth: standard\n   5 files_reviewed: 2\n   6 files_reviewed_list:\n   7   - probe/__init__.py\n   8   - probe/budget.py\n   9 findings:\n  10   critical: 0\n  11   warning: 1\n  12   info: 3\n  13   total: 4\n  14 status: issues_found\n  15 ---\n  16 \n  17 # Phase 01: Code Review Report\n  18 \n  19 **Reviewed:** 2026-10-21\n  20 **Depth:** standard\n  21 **Files Reviewed:** 2\n  22 **Status:** issues_found\n  23 \n  24 ## Summary\n  25 \n  26 `probe/__init__.py` is empty (as the plan specifies). `probe/budget.py` is 5 lines: a typed constant `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120` and `deadline_after(start_secs)` returning `start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`. No bugs, security issues or crashes were found in the code itself. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` was run and reports `Ran 2 tests ... OK`. That result only shows the two existing assertions hold; it does not show the value is the right one (see WR-01, IN-03).\n  27 \n  28 The one real concern is about how the value was chosen, not about the code.\n  29 \n  30 ## Warnings\n  31 \n  32 ### WR-01: Retry budget value was chosen by the orchestrator, not confirmed by a human\n  33 \n  34 **File:** `probe/budget.py:1`\n  35 **Issue:** The plan's Task 1 is a `checkpoint:decision` with `gate=\"blocking-human\"` and `autonomous: false`. The SUMMARY (key-decisions) states 120 s was \"Resolved by the DevFlow orchestrator in an unattended run, NOT confirmed by a human operator.\" The reasoning is checkable against the plan's stated facts: 120 s is greater than the 90 s failover and less than the 240 s pager threshold, and 30 s and 300 s each break one of those. So the value is plausible, but the sign-off the plan required did not happen, and this constant controls how long a worker slot is held per stalled call.\n  36 **Fix:** Have the operator confirm or change the value before this ships. If they change it, edit only the constant in `probe/budget.py`.\n  37 \n  38 ## Info\n  39 \n  40 ### IN-01: `deadline_after` does not state which clock `start_secs` must come from\n  41 \n  42 **File:** `probe/budget.py:4-5`\n  43 **Issue:** The function is a plain addition and works for any float, but the result is only meaningful if the caller compares it against the same clock that produced `start_secs`. A caller passing `time.time()` and later comparing against `time.monotonic()` would get a wrong deadline, with no error. There is no docstring to say which is expected. No caller exists yet, so nothing is broken today.\n  44 **Fix:** Add a one-line docstring, for example: `\"\"\"Return the deadline for start_secs; compare it against the same clock (prefer time.monotonic()).\"\"\"`\n  45 \n  46 ### IN-02: Constant is not marked `Final`\n  47 \n  48 **File:** `probe/budget.py:1`\n  49 **Issue:** `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120` can be rebound by any importer, and `deadline_after` reads the global at call time, so a rebinding changes behaviour process-wide. Type checkers will not flag it.\n  50 **Fix:** `from typing import Final` then `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: Final[int] = 120`. Tests that monkeypatch it would still work at runtime.\n  51 \n  52 ### IN-03: The existing test cannot detect a wrong budget choice\n  53 \n  54 **File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5-10` (context for `prob